<H1 class=3D"title">The inconvenient truth about PVC</H1></DIV>
<DIV class=3D"tabs"></DIV>
<DIV id=3D"pageContent">
<DIV class=3D"node">
<DIV id=3D"articleDate"> By Allan Griff <BR> Published: December 14th, =
2015 </DIV>
<DIV class=3D"like-share">
<SCRIPT type=3D"text/javascript">=0A=
          tweetmeme_style =3D 'compact';=0A=
        </SCRIPT>
        =20
<SCRIPT src=3D"http://tweetmeme.com/i/scripts/button.js" =
type=3D"text/javascript"></SCRIPT>
         <SPAN class=3D"fbshare"><A name=3D"fb_share" =
href=3D"http://www.facebook.com/sharer.php"=20
type=3D"button">Share</A>
<SCRIPT src=3D"http://static.ak.fbcdn.net/connect.php/js/FB.Share" =
type=3D"text/javascript"></SCRIPT>
</SPAN>       </DIV>    		    =20
<DIV class=3D"content">
<P>OK, they don't like us. They think we're poisonous, evil. They won't =
listen=20
to reason (who does, when they feel threatened?). Well, here is my dose =
of=20
reason, but don't expect anyone to listen.</P>
<P>PVC is an unusual plastic. Compared to the other majors, it's the =
only one=20
that is <EM>not</EM> primarily fossil-fuel-based (it's 56% chlorine) =
and,=20
therefore, is flame-resistant; has good chemical resistance; gives the =
most=20
rigidity per unit cost; can also be flexible or rigid, as desired; and =
takes=20
less energy per pound to make and process. And, despite its foes, PVC is =
one of=20
the most extruded plastics, with products ranging from electrical =
conduits and=20
building wire to garden hoses and gummy-worm fish baits.</P>
<P>What's the difference between vinyl and PVC? In common usage, none. =
PVC=20
stands for poly vinyl chloride, and, thus, is one of the vinyl polymers. =
This is=20
a large family, which even includes polyethylene and polystyrene, but =
only a few=20
of the materials retain "vinyl" in their names; of these, PVC is by far =
the most=20
important. As the acronym PVC has received so much negative publicity, =
some=20
users prefer the term vinyl, but it usually refers to good old PVC. =
Other vinyls=20
include PVOH (polyvinyl alcohol), PVDC (polyvinylidene chloride) and =
PVAc=20
(polyvinyl acetate).</P>
<P>All plastics contain additives, even PE, PP and PET, which are =
usually=20
formulated by their producers. And it's the additives that cause most of =
the=20
anxiety in the anti-PVC camp, where they may be (incorrectly) considered =
as part=20
of the PVC itself.</P>
<P>PVC comes in two types: Soft-floppy and hard-rigid. The PVC resin =
itself is=20
hard and rigid, and its hard-rigid applications have stabilizers to =
prevent=20
molecule breakdown, lubricants to make extrusion easier (lower =
viscosity) and=20
other additives such as colorants, as needed. The soft-floppies, on the =
other=20
hand, have these additives but also need 20 to 50% plasticizers to get =
the=20
floppiness. There's very little in between: The properties of a lightly=20
plasticized PVC fit very few big applications. Plasticizers are =
typically=20
high-boiling liquids and may be plant-based, but most of them are =
manufactured=20
compounds, especially phthalates (esters of phthalic acid). The =
Greek-based=20
spelling with <EM>phth</EM> makes them sound "chemical" and contributes =
to the=20
popular aversion to PVC. Never mind that the rigids don't contain any=20
plasticizer at all, nor that there are many FDA-OK non-phthalate =
alternatives,=20
nor that the harm attributed to phthalates is still questionable and, in =
any=20
case, must depend on how much gets into whom and how. The public image =
is still=20
that PVC, and by extension, all plastic, is bad for you.</P>
<P>There is an important distinction between <EM>ortho</EM>phthalates =
and=20
<EM>tere</EM>phthalates that matters in any study of toxicity or legal =
activity,=20
and is well known to chemists, serious environmentalists and even =
lawyers in=20
this area. All phthalates have two usually identical groups attached to =
the=20
phthalic acid ring, but the <EM>orthos</EM> have their two groups next =
to each=20
other, where they may interact and cause the behavior that has raised =
concern.=20
The <EM>teres</EM> have their groups opposite each other, which makes =
them far=20
less likely to interact. The most important use of terephthalates is in =
PET=20
polyester (that's what the T stands for), our #1 in the recycle code, =
used for=20
food and beverages for more than 40 years, and unlikely to receive =
significant=20
opposition. So much for the <EM>phth</EM>. Out of sight, out of mind, =
out of=20
danger.</P>
<P>All extruders of flexible product have to deal with this mess. Do =
they pay=20
more or sacrifice properties to be able to say "phthalate-free?" That =
will=20
depend on the market, of course; makers of dam seals and sewer pipe =
gaskets are=20
less likely to worry than makers of tubing used in food processing =
plants. The=20
medical area has been the main arena of conflict so far, where users of =
tubing=20
and blood bags are under pressure to "get rid of the vinyl."</P>
<P>In the rigid world, the stabilizers are the main worry, and there are =
some=20
bad ones (e.g., those with lead) but it's easy to find stabilizers that =
are=20
FDA-OK for food contact. The PVC-phobes will say that vinyl siding and =
pipe=20
somehow contaminate the air or water, no matter what additives are used, =
and if=20
you can pin them down, they will usually point to chlorine as the =
villain. No=20
matter that the chlorine is tied up as chloride (like salt =3D sodium =
chloride),=20
nor that the resin is very stable in service and not likely to leach =
chlorine=20
anywhere. As for chlorine-containing dioxin, its evil is well known, but =

conditions to transform PVC to dioxin are not seen in its manufacture or =

fabrication, and certainly not in service. The <EM>=E2=80=98phobes</EM> =
have even=20
brought up the BPA issue in attacking PVC, which is meaningless, as BPA =
isn't=20
involved in PVC at all, nor does it contain any chlorine.</P>
<P>By the way, if you ever get attacked by someone who says chlorine is =
bad, ask=20
them what makes plants green, and if they know it's chlorophyll, stop =
there.=20
They may not know that there is no chlorine in chlorophyll.</P>
<P>A word on additives: They usually cost more than the PVC resin, so we =
want to=20
use as little as possible, which means good mixing to get uniform =
compound, and=20
low processing temperatures to reduce the need for stabilizers. This is =
the real=20
justification for the higher cost of twin-screw extruders, which are =
common but=20
not necessary to run PVC. If they can mix well at lower temperatures, =
they use=20
less stabilizer and the savings can pay for the machinery cost =
differential.</P>
<P>In conclusion, if you're already using PVC or considering its use, =
maybe this=20
article can help make you less afraid and more able to defend your =
decision.=20
Know the properties of what you're using, know the ingredients and their =

alternatives, know how to test for stability and viscosity and whatever =
else is=20
important in your use, and work safely, make useful product and keep =
reading=20
<STRONG>PlasticsToday</STRONG>!</P>
<P><EM>Allan Griff is a veteran extrusion engineer, starting out in tech =
service=20
for a major resin supplier, and working on his own now for many years, =
as a=20
consultant, expert witness in law cases, and especially as an educator =
via=20
webinars and seminars, both public and in-house. He wrote the first =
practical=20
extrusion book back in the 1960s as well as the </EM>Plastics Extrusion=20
Operating Manual<EM>, updated almost every year, and available in =
Spanish and=20
French as well as English. Find out more on his website, <SPAN =
style=3D"text-decoration: underline;"><A=20
href=3D"http://www.griffex.com/"=20
target=3D"_blank">http://www.griffex.com/</A></SPAN>, or email him at <A =
href=3D"mailto:algriff@griffex.com"=20
target=3D"_blank">algriff@griffex.com</A>.</EM></P>
